# The kept-state pool: a byte budget with hand-back, and a per-conversation quota. Both off by default.
# (1) The pool counted bookmark slots but never weighed what they own, and dropping one returned nothing: a few
#     long conversations drove the hosts to 0.1 GB free. TF_GLM_KEPT_BYTES_GIB (default 0: no cap) bounds the kept
#     states together by bytes, least-recently-used past that; with it set, freed device memory is handed back to
#     the driver once 512 MB has piled up (returning per drop measurably slowed decoding).
# (2) A bookmark after every model reply let one long conversation eat every slot, and conversations taking
#     turns kept chilling each other out: TF_GLM_KEEP_PER_CHAT (default 0: no limit) makes a conversation keep a
#     few of its own at its turn boundaries at most, while the shared system-prompt state and mid-prefill pauses do
#     not count against the quota.
# With the spill tier (0088) on, neither limit writes the states it drops to disk: they are superseded turns or
# over a memory budget, not states leaving a full pool (the entry cap's drops still spill).
# /health reports the per-conversation state table (kept_mix) and the byte budget (kept_bytes, kept_bytes_cap).
# Authored by Thomas Wade (@ThomasWadeZ), PR #65 (Apache-2.0); the PR's third part, copying the resumed rows instead
# of cutting the donor, is patch 0071 already and is not part of this patch. Rebased onto 0071-0088 and made
# off by default by the maintainer.

